#
# Caddy in front of pictorio-db, so that the desktop app and the admin pages use HTTPS. Caddy gets and renews
# the certificates by itself (https://caddyserver.com/docs/automatic-https).
#
# 1. Let only Caddy reach pictorio-db, and let pictorio-db believe Caddy's X-Forwarded-* headers:
#    - Container (deploy/pictorio-db.container): PublishPort=127.0.0.1:8737:8737 and
#      Environment=PICTORIO_TRUSTED_PROXIES=*. The container sees Caddy's requests as coming from the host's
#      own address, so it trusts any; only Caddy can reach the port.
#    - pipx install: nothing to change. It listens on 127.0.0.1 and trusts 127.0.0.1 by default.
# 2. Use one of the two sites below: copy it to /etc/caddy/Caddyfile, put in your name and reload Caddy:
#    sudo systemctl reload caddy

# A name on the internet, e.g. a dynamic-DNS name for your home connection, with ports 80 and 443 forwarded to
# this computer. Caddy gets a free certificate from Let's Encrypt.
photos.example.org {
	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8737
}

# Or only in your home network: Caddy is its own certificate authority. Every device that connects must trust
# Caddy's root certificate once; it is in Caddy's data folder, e.g.
# /var/lib/caddy/.local/share/caddy/pki/authorities/local/root.crt
#pictorio.home.arpa {
#	tls internal
#	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8737
#}
